# AI in this course — what to expect

Short guide for **students** in this course. Your published **syllabus and
__LMS__** win if anything here differs for a specific term.

## Preamble

**AI** is part of professional life. Policy will keep changing; the skill of
**using AI as a tool while owning your work** will not go away.

Teaching full **agentic engineering** as a product skill is **not** the default
objective of every course—unless your syllabus says otherwise. Learning **how
to leverage AI without outsourcing understanding** is.

## What AI is for here

You may use AI tools to **support learning**, for example:

- Explaining a concept in different words
- Helping to work through a concept or task that you're struggling with
- Helping debug **your** code or notebook (with sensitive data removed)
- Brainstorming approaches or study questions
- Checking your work against a rubric **after** you have attempted it

You may use AI tools to **support productivity**, for example:

- Scaffolding a document or notebook **layout** (consider saving your initial prompt if the course asks)
- Cleaning wording after *your* analysis is done
- Generating practice problems for yourself

**Do not outsource your critical thinking.** AI is **not** a substitute for
understanding the material, methods, or ethical constraints of the assignment.

## What remains your responsibility

- **You own everything submitted** and must be able to defend decisions if asked.
- Graded work should reflect **your** reasoning unless an assignment explicitly allows more.
- If you cannot explain or re-create what you submitted, you are not ready to turn it in.
- You are responsible for errors, unsafe recommendations, and policy violations—even if a tool suggested them.

## Allowed vs careful vs not OK (defaults)

| Usually OK | Be careful | Not OK (defaults) |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Concept questions, definitions | Large paste of your draft for “rewrite it all” | Submitting AI-generated work as solely yours when the goal is *your* skill |
| Explaining an error message | Using AI on data that is not clearly public | Putting **restricted** data in consumer AI tools |
| Practice problems you invent | Relying on AI for literature claims without checking sources | Uploading classmates’ work, answer keys, or private course materials |
| Rubric self-check after your attempt | — | Ignoring a stricter rule on *this* course’s syllabus / handbook |

Individual courses may tighten or loosen this table. Always read the syllabus
for your section.

## Data, privacy, and restricted sources

- Do **not** paste PHI, real student records, or **credentialed** extracts into consumer AI tools unless the instructor has named an **approved** path.
- Prefer **public sample files** from the course repo (e.g. under `data/public/`).
- Credentials, API keys, and login details never go into chats.

If you are unsure whether a file is allowed, **ask the instructor before**
using an external AI tool on it.

## Honesty and disclosure

- If the syllabus or assignment asks you to **disclose** AI use, do so briefly (what tool, what for).
- Fabricating sources, results, or “I ran this” when you did not is an integrity issue—with or without AI.

## How this relates to coding agents

Some courses include files such as `AGENTS.md` or skill folders for **optional**
coding agents. Those files guide the *tools*. They do not replace this page or
the syllabus.

You are not required to use local agents unless the course says so.

## When policies conflict

1. **Syllabus / __LMS__ for your term**
2. This document (this repository’s copy)
3. Informal advice from tools or classmates

## Questions

Ask the instructor or TA early—especially about data and disclosure—before a
deadline makes the answer urgent.
